    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 64\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 10470-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  in  relation  to  directing  the commissioner of health to\n      conduct a study of the  delivery  of  ambulatory  care  on  Staten\n      Island\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would direct the Commissioner of Health to conduct a study\non the current situation and expected future trends associated with  the\ndelivery  of  primary  care,  urgent  care, and commonly used ambulatory\nhealth care services on Staten Island. The study would  examine  a  wide\nrange of entities and their impact on the delivery, quality, accessibil-\nity,  and  costs  of  ambulatory care on Staten Island. The Commissioner\nwould be required to publish the findings from the study on the  Depart-\nment  of  Health's website and to transmit those findings and any recom-\nmendations to the Governor and the Legislature within one year from  the\ndate the bill takes effect.\n \n  In  accordance with Chapter 369 of the Laws of 2013, the Department of\nHealth published a report in 2017 that looked at current innovations  in\nthe  delivery  of health care services, examining retail clinics, urgent\ncare providers and major physician practices. The  report  explored  the\nimpact  of  these  respective types of entities on the delivery, quality\nand cost of health care in the respective  communities  and  regions  in\nwhich they were found.\n \n  Furthermore, this bill would mandate the Department to conduct a study\nwithout  an  established funding source. Since the costs imposed by this\nstudy must be considered during budget discussions, I am constrained  to\nveto  this bill. However, in recognition of the concerns in the communi-\nty, I am directing the Department to immediately review the situation in\nState Island to ensure that there are  adequate  services  in  light  of\nurgent care closures from COVID-19.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 64,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2020
   }
